 1 & 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z &=1 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nStarting with the second equation $z=1,$ we substitute into the first equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+3(1) &=1\\,⇒\\,x=-2-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2-y \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-y \\\\ y \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d865bfb4-6ff5-577a-a992-c481af8842b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with Two Pivots"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105605 :question/id #uuid "b934f229-5082-5e68-887c-6d3da20f173f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4708" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx + y + 3z = 1\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx = 1-y-3z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-y-3z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-y-3z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this is the equation of a plane in $\\mathbb R^3$, and any point on the plane is a solution to the system."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105645 :question/id #uuid "bab65b1f-cf10-58ea-be87-3a3999722d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6202" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 19 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 19 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y-6z &=7 \\\\ 0x+y+2z &=4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 3,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f6a868-46fe-5529-a464-b6f3e0243d30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Singular 3x3 System of Equations That Has No Solution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1374 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :topic/title "Solving 3x3 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}]}
